WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Speaker 1: So we're continuing our conversations&nbsp;
with a number of different Republicans running in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.000
the 2022 midterm elections. Today, we're going&nbsp;
to be speaking with John Gibbs. John Gibbs is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.080
Trump endorsed Republican, the former assistant&nbsp;
secretary for community planning and development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.880
at HUD in the Trump administration and is&nbsp;
running in Michigan's third congressional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.200
district. The incumbent in that race is&nbsp;
Peter Meyer. It's great to have you on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.840
John. I really appreciate your time.
Speaker 2: Thanks for having me on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.400
David. I really appreciate it.
Speaker 1: So let's start with something really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:38.400
easy just to kind of get started. Donald Trump&nbsp;
took a bit of issue with your opponent's last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.960
name, having a silent Jay in it. He seemed not&nbsp;
to like that at a recent rally. You take no issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.880
with the spelling of your opponent's name, right?
Speaker 2: Well, I think the difference is I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.560
from Michigan, and so we grew up seeing&nbsp;
the supermarket chain and whatnot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.680
So we're really used to it. But I can&nbsp;
see how someone who's not from here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.360
like President Trump, would not be familiar&nbsp;
with that spelling or how I pronounce it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.960
you know, that's how I look at it.
Speaker 1: Beautiful. Okay. So I want to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:10.240
with what is leading you to run. Most people&nbsp;
who choose to run say, well, to some degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.880
I mean, people in media and people in politics,&nbsp;
in a sense, there's a narcissistic element,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.280
right? Like, if you're in media and you want&nbsp;
to talk, you have to on some level think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.120
what I'm saying is at least as interesting as&nbsp;
what anybody else is going to have to say. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.760
you're going to run for office, to some degree,&nbsp;
you believe you can do a better job than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.800
person that's there. So tell me about that. What&nbsp;
problems do you see that you think you can fix?&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.240
Speaker 2: Well, I mean, the list goes on and&nbsp;
on. But we have an inflation which is causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.440
gas prices to, you know, skyrocket through the&nbsp;
roof. We have empty shelves at stores and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.440
have high prices at grocery stores as well&nbsp;
for basic things like chicken or pork chop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.160
whatever it might be. This is having a huge impact&nbsp;
on the middle class and working class folks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.440
especially the poor and minorities, really&nbsp;
got to step up and do something about this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.240
And that's just a message that doesn't talk&nbsp;
about Afghanistan and the botched withdrawal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.240
and Ukraine and what's going on there. So&nbsp;
I think there's just a very long list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.720
issues that we need to address. And I think&nbsp;
very humbly, based on my experience in D.C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.480
for four years and how an administration, I got&nbsp;
to learn the machinery of how government works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.280
I was there for longer than my primary opponent&nbsp;
has been there. So I've got more experience than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.400
I have in that. Also, I'm a regular guy. I'm not&nbsp;
from a, you know, super, super loaded background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.640
So I know what it's like to connect with regular&nbsp;
folks. So I think I've got the best of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.200
worlds there. But David, you do have a point.&nbsp;
I mean, you have people who are narcissistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.280
and this profession does attract those kind&nbsp;
of people. And I myself, after administration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.520
was not planning on going into politics. I was, in&nbsp;
fact, planning on becoming a regular person again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.960
you know, maybe doing some work for&nbsp;
a think tank or something and just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.920
you know, getting out of the spotlight for a&nbsp;
bit. But then some friends, you know, for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.200
said, hey, why don't you go back home to Michigan&nbsp;
and, you know, take a run against this Peter Meyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.600
guy who voted to impeach Trump. And it took me a&nbsp;
while. I thought about it a lot. I prayed about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.560
it. I made sure I had my ducks in a row and&nbsp;
ended up diving in head first to do it. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.680
it's not really necessarily. I was thinking I&nbsp;
was kind of looking forward to being a regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.640
person again. So yeah, I mean, it does really&nbsp;
take something to drop out here and do this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.400
It's very busy, you know, you're going to&nbsp;
get attacked and there's long hours and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.600
there's scrutiny and things. But I do really&nbsp;
think it's worth it because, you know, we need the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.440
leadership in there, which is lacking right now.
Speaker 1: So you mentioned three issues there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.720
The first one was was inflation and&nbsp;
gas prices and product product prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.760
That's that's something that's happening globally&nbsp;
right now. There's a number of issues there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.680
As one of 435 members of Congress in the&nbsp;
United States, what would you do to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.480
the issue of inflation, which is a global one?
Speaker 2: Well, it's a global issue to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.720
degree. But a lot of this money that's being&nbsp;
printed and the spending that's happening does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.120
have global impact because the investors take that&nbsp;
money and they invest it all around the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.440
So I do think the Europeans certainly&nbsp;
have their share of guilt in this for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.800
